---
/**
 * O nás   a centred column of type standing in a clearing.
 *
 * Copy is the paragraph from the current homepage, which is correct as written  
 * KEEP, docs/01-content.md §4.2.
 *
 * WHAT WAS ON THE RIGHT, AND WHY NOTHING IS NOW
 * This was a split: text on the left, a panel on the right. The panel held a
 * photograph of the terrace, then a flat-vector drawing of the valley. Both had
 * the same problem   there was nothing for them to say. The paragraph already
 * places the cottage, and every other section on this page carries a photograph
 * of its own subject, so a second picture of the same valley was filling a
 * rectangle rather than answering a question.
 *
 * A panel with nothing to put in it is a panel that should not be there. So the
 * section is now one measure of type in the middle of the page, with a stand of
 * spruce at each end and a gap between them (Clearing.astro). This is the first
 * prose on the page and the only place that says where the cottage IS; setting it
 * alone, centred, is the strongest thing that can be done to it.
 *
 * ⚠ The centring is deliberate and it is the exception, not the rule.
 * docs/02-design-system.md §3 sets the page left-of-centre with the right third
 * open. Two places break it: this one and the inquiry form. Both are moments
 * where the page stops and addresses the reader directly. A third would make the
 * asymmetry look like an accident rather than a decision.
 */
import Clearing from '../decor/Clearing.astro';
---

<section class="section onas" aria-labelledby="o-nas-nadpis">
  <Clearing />

  <div class="wrap onas-inner" data-reveal>
    <h2 id="o-nas-nadpis">O nás</h2>
    <p class="lead">
      Chalupa Heřmanka se nachází v malé obci Heřmanice u Králík, která je situována
      v malebném údolí pod masivem Králického Sněžníku. Jedná se o kompletně
      zrekonstruovaný dvoupodlažní objekt, zasazený do zalesněné stráně. Chalupa
      k pronajmutí poskytuje ubytování pro 2 až 15 osob ve 3 ložnicích.
    </p>
    <p class="more">
      <a href="/ubytovani" class="link-more">
        <span>Více o nás</span>
      </a>
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

<style>
  .onas {
    /* The measure, and the one number the stands are sized against   see the
       gutter arithmetic further down. It is a variable because it is read in two
       places and two hand-typed 46rems would drift. */
    --measure: 46rem;
    position: relative;
    /*
     * A backstop, not a layout tool. Nothing is supposed to reach it: the stands
     * are fitted to their own drawing and capped to the gutter below, so this
     * only catches a future mistake before it spills into the neighbouring band.
     */
    overflow: hidden;
    background-color: var(--color-omitka);
  }

  /*
   * The type sits above the drawing in the stacking order but never over it in
   * space: the stands are outside the measure, and the measure is centred.
   */
  .onas-inner {
    position: relative;
    max-width: var(--measure);
    text-align: center;
  }

  .onas-inner > * {
    margin-inline: auto;
  }

  h2 {
    color: var(--color-mosaz-tmava);
  }

  .lead {
    margin-top: 1.5rem;
    /* Shorter than the 65ch default: centred text is harder to track back to the
       start of the next line, so the measure has to be tighter than it would be
       for the same paragraph set ragged-right. */
    max-width: 54ch;
    font-size: var(--text-lead);
  }

  .more {
    margin-top: 2rem;
  }

  /*
   * The stands.
   *
   * ⚠ REMOVED BELOW 64rem, not shrunk   `display: none`, so nothing is painted
   * and nothing is downloaded twice. It is not that they would not fit: below
   * that width the measure fills the page, so there is no clearing left for them
   * to stand at the edge of and they would end up behind the type. A phone gets
   * the centred column on its own, which is the version of this section that
   * never needed help.
   */
  .onas :global(.clearing) {
    display: none;
  }

  @media (width >= 64rem) {
    /*
     * The stands are taller than the paragraph, so THEY set the height of the
     * band and the type is centred against them. Padding alone left the type at
     * the top with a field of empty stone under it.
     */
    .onas {
      --stand-h: clamp(210px, 26vw, 390px);
      /*
       * What the stand can ACTUALLY draw, which is not always what it asked for:
       * the box is capped to the gutter (see below), so from 64rem to about
       * 76rem the width binds first and the trees come out shorter than
       * `--stand-h`. Reserving the full height there left a field of empty stone
       * over their tips.
       *
       * 1 is the stands' own aspect   roughly 477 �  470 and 414 �  430, the
       * viewBoxes Clearing.astro measures from the placed trees. It is an
       * estimate for WHITESPACE only; nothing is clipped if it drifts. `100vw`
       * rather than `100%` because a percentage in `min-height` resolves against
       * height.
       */
      --stand-drawn: min(var(--stand-h), max(0px, (100vw - var(--measure)) / 2 + 1.5rem));
      display: grid;
      align-content: center;
      min-height: calc(var(--stand-drawn) + 11rem);
      padding-block: 5.5rem;
    }

    /*
     * ---- THE NO-CLIP CONTRACT, WHICH IS TWO RULES AND BOTH ARE LOAD-BEARING --
     *
     * 1. `height` is the drawing's AMBITION and `max-width` is its permission.
     *    The stand is `preserveAspectRatio="…YMax meet"` (Clearing.astro), so
     *    whichever of the two binds first, the drawing scales down uniformly
     *    inside the box, stays pinned to the outer edge and the ground line, and
     *    is never cropped and never squashed. On a wide screen the height binds
     *    and the stand is full size; from 64rem to about 76rem the gutter binds
     *    and the trees are simply smaller. Neither case cuts anything.
     *
     * 2. The gutter is (page − measure) / 2. The extra 1.5rem is borrowed from
     *    the measure's own 2rem of `.wrap` padding   no glyph is ever set there,
     *    so the outermost crown may lean into it, and the remaining 0.5rem is
     *    the gap that keeps it off the text.
     *
     * `max()` rather than a bare `calc()` on purpose: a negative `max-width` is
     * an invalid declaration and would be DROPPED   i.e. the guard would vanish
     * at exactly the width where it matters. Clamped at zero it fails closed.
     *
     * Vertical clearance is the section's own `min-height` above: stand + 11rem
     * against the stand + the 2.75rem it is lifted by, so the top of the drawing
     * always has room.
     */
    .onas :global(.clearing) {
      display: block;
      position: absolute;
      /* Not 0. Sitting on the section edge, the overlapping skirts merge into one
         hard-cut block against the band below and read as a notch in the layout
         rather than as trees. A strip of clear ground under them fixes it. */
      bottom: 2.75rem;
      height: var(--stand-h);
      width: auto;
      max-width: max(0px, calc((100% - var(--measure)) / 2 + 1.5rem));
      pointer-events: none;
    }

    .onas :global(.clearing-left) {
      left: 0;
    }

    .onas :global(.clearing-right) {
      right: 0;
    }
  }
</style>
